=== L2 Characters: Sephy, Callum and Their Families ===
id: d2a432ed-0421-4013-892e-69932573fdbd
slug: characters-sephy-callum-and-their-families
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:07.804395+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Two teenagers, their mothers, and the political tensions that pull them apart.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Building Character in a Divided World</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">In Cooke&rsquo;s adaptation, every character carries the weight of their social position &mdash; Cross or nought &mdash; in their body, their costume and their <dfn class="term" data-def="The distance and spatial relationship between performers on stage, communicating power, intimacy or conflict.">proxemics</dfn>. Understanding character in this play means understanding that identity is not just personal but systemic: how a character moves through the world, who they stand close to, who they are permitted to touch, is regulated by a state hierarchy. This means that performance choices about posture, gesture and <dfn class="term" data-def="The distance a performer maintains from others, used to signal status, relationship and power.">personal space</dfn> are politically loaded in a way that would not be true of a purely domestic drama.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Sephy Hadley: Privilege and Its Costs</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Sephy is a Cross teenager &mdash; daughter of Kamal Hadley, a senior Cross politician equivalent to a Home Secretary. She begins the play idealistic and naive, genuinely unable to see how the privileges of her class have shaped her. A performer playing Sephy in the opening scenes might physicalize this through an open, unselfconscious <dfn class="term" data-def="The way a performer holds their body, suggesting character, status and emotional state.">posture</dfn> &mdash; taking up space freely, moving without caution through the shared world of childhood. As the play progresses and Sephy begins to understand what the system requires of her, a director might ask the performer to pull this openness back: a gradual closing of the body, a wariness in the gaze, a loss of the easy movement she once had.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Sephy&rsquo;s journey is one of radicalisation in reverse: she starts privileged and ends broken. For a lighting designer, this arc offers a clear visual opportunity. In the early scenes, Sephy might be lit with a warm overhead wash, the colour of sunlight, suggesting her unexamined confidence. By the play&rsquo;s final sequences, a shift to a cooler, more isolating <dfn class="term" data-def="The overall quality and colour of light illuminating the stage at a given moment.">lighting state</dfn> &mdash; perhaps a single profile lantern from a steep angle &mdash; could signal how utterly her world has contracted.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what two things does Sephy's character arc move between? Write a sentence about how a performer might physicalize that change.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Sephy Hadley is a Cross teenager whose arc moves from naive idealism to hardened experience. Her father Kamal is a senior Cross politician; her mother Jasmine is outwardly respectable but privately alcoholic. Sephy&rsquo;s privilege is the play&rsquo;s central irony: she cannot see what it costs others until it is too late.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Callum McGregor: Denied and Radicalised</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Callum is a nought teenager &mdash; bright, academically able, yet denied access to the &ldquo;good&rdquo; Cross schools until one limited integration scheme briefly opens a door. His character carries the play&rsquo;s most immediate political charge: he is the proof that noughts are not inferior, simply excluded. A performer playing Callum must navigate the tension between Callum&rsquo;s private warmth (especially in the scenes with Sephy) and his public caution &mdash; the learned habit of making himself smaller in Cross spaces.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">This physical self-policing is one of the play&rsquo;s most powerful performance opportunities. In Cross spaces &mdash; the school, formal settings &mdash; a director might ask the performer playing Callum to reduce <dfn class="term" data-def="The use of body and movement to express character, emotion and relationship on stage.">physical expressiveness</dfn>: keep the arms closer to the body, reduce gesture, avoid direct eye contact with Cross characters. When Callum is alone with Sephy, or in nought spaces, this tension can release, and the performer can find the fuller, freer physicality of someone no longer performing safety. This contrast in itself communicates the structural violence of the play&rsquo;s world.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Callum as Performer: The School Integration Scene</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">The first day at the Cross school is one of the play&rsquo;s key staging moments. As a performer playing Callum arriving at the school, you might begin your entrance upstage, walking towards the audience through a gauntlet of staring Cross students placed at stage left and stage right. Your pace might slow progressively &mdash; each step taken with deliberate care, a pause before crossing any threshold. Your vocal delivery in any lines spoken here could be clipped, quieter than usual, a small register suggesting the suppression of the fuller self. The <dfn class="term" data-def="The arrangement of performers in the performance space, reflecting meaning and relationship.">blocking</dfn> of this arrival &mdash; Callum isolated at centre stage while the ensemble closes in from the sides &mdash; would communicate the surveillance of the Cross institution without a single line of dialogue needing to explain it.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">The Mothers: Jasmine and Meggie</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Jasmine Hadley and Meggie McGregor are mirror characters: both mothers, both women shaped by the system, both trying to protect their children. But the system has shaped them in opposite directions. Jasmine is outwardly poised &mdash; a Cross woman in a comfortable home &mdash; but privately alcoholic, her composure a performance that costs her enormously. A costume designer might give Jasmine immaculate fitted clothing in neutral tones or cream, signifying the Cross ideal of restrained elegance, while using subtle visual cues &mdash; a slightly misaligned accessory, a drink glass always within reach &mdash; to suggest the cracks beneath.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Meggie McGregor, once Jasmine&rsquo;s nanny and now a nought woman struggling after her husband Ryan&rsquo;s arrest, carries exhaustion as a costume and physical signature. A designer working on Meggie&rsquo;s costume might choose worn, darker fabrics &mdash; colours that have faded with washing &mdash; and a silhouette that is practical rather than decorative, communicating a life spent in service and in survival. Where Jasmine&rsquo;s <dfn class="term" data-def="The overall shape and outline of a costume, communicating character status, period and personality.">costume silhouette</dfn> speaks of leisure, Meggie&rsquo;s speaks of labour.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Test yourself: write down one specific costume choice for Jasmine and one for Meggie, and explain what each communicates about their status in the play's world.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Jasmine Hadley and Meggie McGregor are structural mirrors: both mothers, both shaped by the racial hierarchy in opposite ways. Ryan McGregor (Callum&rsquo;s father) is a nought activist arrested by the state; his absence is as significant as his presence in shaping Callum&rsquo;s trajectory towards the Liberation Militia.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Supporting Characters: Jude, Lynette and the State</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Jude McGregor (Callum&rsquo;s brother) is radicalised by the death of his sister Lynette and becomes a Liberation Militia operative. Where Callum is drawn in reluctantly, Jude chooses violence as a response to grief. In performance terms, Jude offers a contrast to Callum: where Callum pulls inward in Cross spaces, Jude pushes outward &mdash; a reckless, outward-projected physicality that signals his abandonment of self-preservation. Lynette, mentally ill after a racist attack, is a character whose presence serves as an early signal of the violence the system is capable of, before it turns on the McGregor family directly.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n17">State officials &mdash; interrogators, prison officers &mdash; are typically <dfn class="term" data-def="A technique in which one performer plays more than one role within the same production.">multi-roled</dfn> in productions of this play. This is both a practical casting economy and a political statement: the system&rsquo;s agents are interchangeable, institutional rather than individual. A director using multi-roling for the state officials might ask performers to adopt a shared <dfn class="term" data-def="A stylised gesture or posture that expresses a character's social position and attitude, as theorised by Bertolt Brecht.">gestus</dfn> &mdash; a particular physical bearing, a precise angle of authority &mdash; that marks out their function regardless of which character they are currently playing.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: &lsquo;Black and White Costumes&rsquo; as a Design Answer</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">A very common exam error is to answer costume design questions in Noughts and Crosses with &lsquo;Crosses would wear black and noughts would wear white.&rsquo; This is too simple and misses the play&rsquo;s complexity. The play does not work with a straightforward black/white binary in costume &mdash; it works with coded markers of class, formality, status and access. A more sophisticated design answer would specify: the <em>fabric</em> (fine wool or silk for Cross characters versus cotton or synthetic blends for noughts), the <em>fit</em> (tailored versus off-the-peg), the <em>colour palette</em> (privileged neutrals and jewel tones for Crosses, muted desaturated colours for noughts), and the <em>condition</em> of the clothing (new and pressed versus worn and faded). Explain what each choice communicates about the hierarchy, not just that there is a hierarchy.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Sephy moves from naive privilege to hardened experience; Callum from suppressed possibility to tragic loss. Both character arcs offer clear performance tracks: the opening physicality should contrast starkly with the closing, and a designer can track this through lighting state and costume condition.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Jasmine and Meggie are structural mirrors &mdash; mothers shaped in opposite directions by the racial hierarchy. Costume choices for each (silhouette, fabric, condition) communicate their status without a word of explanation.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Multi-roling of state officials is both a practical economy and a political choice: the system&rsquo;s agents share a gestus, communicating institutional rather than individual power.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 8-mark performer question, the most frequent error is writing &lsquo;I would say it angrily&rsquo; without specifying <em>how</em> &mdash; what volume, what pace, what pitch, what physical choice in the body at that moment. For Callum, think about the tension between what he feels and what he can safely show: a performer playing Callum in the school integration scene might keep the voice controlled and quiet (volume reduced, pace measured) while the physical tension shows in the hands or jaw. Name the moment, name the vocal choice, name the physical choice, and explain what each communicates to the audience. Similarly, for designer questions, never write &lsquo;period costume&rsquo; or &lsquo;dark lighting&rsquo; &mdash; name the fabric, the silhouette, the lantern, the gel colour, and explain the effect.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
